## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates mastery of several advanced graph techniques: DAG dynamic programming, multi-criteria optimization, path reconstruction, and cycle handling. It tests the ability to reason about edge attributes with different behaviors (skip-able vs mandatory latency) and implement lexicographic optimization where hops are prioritized over latency.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting for efficient DAG processing
- Lexicographic multi-objective optimization (hops first, latency second)
- Predecessor-based path reconstruction
- Cycle detection using DFS and graph condensation
- Edge attribute handling with conditional weight computation

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(graph, s, t) {
  // Assumes graph is a DAG. Each edge has: latency w, skip boolean
  const order = topologicalSort(graph);
  const best = new Map();
  const pred = new Map();

  best.set(s, [0, 0]); // [hops, latency]

  for (const u of order) {
    if (!best.has(u)) continue;
    const [hops, latency] = best.get(u);

    for (const {to: v, latency: w, skip} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      const cand = [hops + 1, latency + (skip ? 0 : w)];
      const current = best.get(v);
      if (!current || lexicographicallySmaller(cand, current)) {
        best.set(v, cand);
        pred.set(v, u);
      }
    }
  }

  return reconstructPath(pred, s, t);
}

function detectCycles(graph) {
  const visited = new Set();
  const recStack = new Set();

  function dfs(u) {
    if (recStack.has(u)) return true; // Cycle detected
    if (visited.has(u)) return false;

    visited.add(u);
    recStack.add(u);

    for (const {to: v} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      if (dfs(v)) return true;
    }

    recStack.delete(u);
    return false;
  }

  return Array.from(graph.nodes()).some(dfs);
}
```

## Adaptation for Cyclic Graphs
If cycles are present, first detect them using DFS with a recursion stack. For graphs with cycles, apply strongly connected components (SCC) decomposition to create a condensation DAG, then solve on the condensed graph. Alternatively, use Dijkstra's algorithm modified for lexicographic optimization where the edge weight is the tuple (1, w or 0 based on skip).

## Why This Is Asked
This problem assesses mastery of graph algorithms on DAGs, including topological sorting, feasibility verification under ordered constraints, and shortest-path computation.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort for DAGs
- DAG shortest-path DP (O(|V| + |E|))
- Feasibility checking with ordered mandatory edges
- Path reconstruction via predecessor pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestConstrainedPath(G, S, T, M) {
  const topo = topoSort(G);
  const idx = (v) => topo.indexOf(v);

  const canReach = (from, to) => {
    if (idx(from) >= idx(to)) return false;
    const reachable = new Set([from]);
    for (let i = idx(from); i < idx(to); i++) {
      for (const edge of G.adj[topo[i]]) {
        if (idx(edge.to) < idx(to)) reachable.add(edge.to);
      }
    }
    return reachable.has(to);
  };

  const points = [S, ...M.map(e => e[0]), ...M.map(e => e[1]), T];
  for (let i = 0; i < points.length - 1; i++) {
    if (!canReach(points[i], points[i + 1])) return null;
  }

  const segmentCost = (src, dst) => {
    const dist = {}; dist[src] = 0;
    for (let i = idx(src); i < idx(dst); i++) {
      const u = topo[i];
      for (const edge of G.adj[u]) {
        if (idx(edge.to) < idx(dst)) {
          if (dist[u] + edge.w < (dist[edge.to] ?? Infinity)) {
            dist[edge.to] = dist[u] + edge.w;
          }
        }
      }
    }
    return dist[dst];
  };

  let total = 0;
  const fullPath = [S];
  for (const [u, v, w] of M) {
    total += w;
    const seg = segmentCost(fullPath[fullPath.length - 1], u);
    if (seg === undefined) return null;
    total += seg;
  }
  const finalSeg = segmentCost(M[M.length - 1][1], T);
  if (finalSeg === undefined) return null;
  return total + finalSeg;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Duplicates are common in real data and inputs are often skewed. A 3-way partition quicksort directly handles the middle bucket of equal keys, avoiding repeated swaps of equal elements and preventing degeneracy.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch national flag style) for handling duplicates
- Pivot selection strategies (median-of-three to reduce worst-case risk)
- Introsort/hybrid switching to guarantee worst-case bounds
- In-place sorting with O(log n) recursion depth

## Code Example

```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  while (lo < hi) {
    let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;
    const pivot = a[lo];
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (a[i] < pivot) { [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (a[i] > pivot) { [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt; // tail recursion on the right side
  }
  return a;
}
```

